flowchart TD
  %% author: Brad W. Setser | outlet: CFR Follow the Money | date: 2020-10-05
  %% title: Record Chinese Bilateral Surpluses With the United States Are Not Mirrored in the U.S. Trade Data
  %% url: https://www.cfr.org/articles/record-chinese-bilateral-surpluses-united-states-are-not-mirrored-us-trade-data
  %% topics: China, US, bilateral trade, statistics, IMF | genre: blog / data critique

  subgraph L0["Level 0 · Question"]
    Q["`**Question**
      Is China's bilateral trade surplus with the United States really back at a record level, and how should conflicting U.S. and Chinese trade data be interpreted?`"]
  end

  subgraph L1["Level 1 · Priors & assumptions"]
    P1["<b>The bilateral data normally diverge predictably</b><br/>U.S. imports historically exceed Chinese exports, largely because of Hong Kong"]
    P2["<b>A break in a stable data relationship is informative</b><br/>The signal is the reversal of the long-standing discrepancy"]
  end

  subgraph L2["Level 2 · Evidence"]
    D1["<b>Historical benchmark</b><br/>U.S. imports from China normally exceed China's reported exports to the U.S."]
    D2["<b>2020 pattern reverses</b><br/>Chinese exports now exceed U.S. imports in monthly and trailing 3-month data"]
    D3["<b>July comparison flips sign</b><br/>2018: 41.6 vs 47; 2019: 38.9 vs 41.4;<br/>2020: 43.7 vs 40.7 billion"]
    D4["<b>The two datasets imply different trade stories</b><br/>Chinese data show a record surplus; U.S. data do not show a record deficit"]
  end

  subgraph L3["Level 3 · Mechanisms"]
    M1["<b>Tariffs encourage U.S. import under-reporting</b><br/>Importers have an incentive to understate Chinese imports"]
  end

  subgraph L4["Level 4 · Intermediate claims"]
    C1["<b>The old Hong Kong explanation is no longer enough</b><br/>It explains the historical gap, not the new sign reversal"]
    C2["<b>The discrepancy likely reflects reporting behavior</b><br/>The measured bilateral gap may not map cleanly to the underlying flow"]
  end

  subgraph L5["Level 5 · Conclusion"]
    K["<b>Main claim: U.S. imports are probably undercounted</b><br/>Tariff incentives are the most parsimonious explanation,<br/>but the discrepancy is not yet conclusively resolved"]
  end

  subgraph L6["Level 6 · Dialectic"]
    X["<b>Alternative explanation</b><br/>Chinese exporters may be overstating exports"]
    QF1["<b>Timing caveat</b><br/>The reversal appears in monthly and 3-month data, not yet in the 12-month sum"]
    QF2["<b>Evidence caveat</b><br/>More investigation is needed before reaching a firm conclusion"]
  end

  P1 --> C1
  D1 --> C1

  P2 --> C2
  D2 --> C2
  D3 --> C2
  D4 --> C2

  C1 --> M1
  C2 --> M1
  M1 --> K

  D2 -.->|limited by| QF1
  K -.->|not conclusive| QF2

  Q ~~~ P1 & P2
  P1 ~~~ D1
  P2 ~~~ D2 & D3 & D4

